\section{Related Work}

Collaborative filtering is a widely adopted technique for predicting user preferences based on historical data from similar users. It is generally categorized into two types: memory-based and model-based. Memory-based methods, such as user-based and item-based approaches, rely on the assumption that users with similar past behaviors will have similar future preferences. In contrast, model-based methods, including matrix factorization, construct a model to predict user preferences.

Matrix factorization decomposes the user-item interaction matrix into two lower-dimensional matrices representing users and items, respectively. This technique has been extensively researched and applied in various recommendation systems. However, matrix factorization often struggles to capture complex user preferences and item characteristics. To address this limitation, deep neural networks have been introduced to learn user and item representations.

