\section{Introduction}

Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a cornerstone of artificial intelligence, enabling agents to learn optimal behaviors through trial and error. However, applying RL to complex stochastic systems with large state spaces remains challenging. To address this, we propose a hierarchical reinforcement learning (HRL) method that exploits the system's structure to improve learning efficiency. Our approach employs a two-level architecture: a high-level controller that determines the overall strategy and a low-level controller that carries out specific actions. We show that this hierarchy can be acquired via a two-stage learning process. First, the high-level controller learns to choose suitable low-level controllers. Second, the low-level controller learns to perform the actions specified by the high-level controller. We evaluate this method on a multi-agent system and demonstrate that it outperforms a standard RL algorithm. Additionally, we provide a theoretical analysis of the method's convergence. The source code can be found at https://github.com/zhengyuan1995/HRL.

