\section{Introduction}

Tensor completion addresses the challenge of recovering a complete tensor from a limited set of observed entries. This problem is prevalent in numerous applications, including image and video processing, medical imaging, and recommendation systems. A primary assumption underlying tensor completion is that the target tensor possesses a low-rank structure. Consequently, the task involves reconstructing the full tensor by exploiting this low-rank property.

The low-rank tensor completion problem can be expressed as:
\begin{equation}
\min_{\mathcal{X}} \|\mathcal{X}\|_{*} \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\mathcal{X}) = \mathcal{Y},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{X} \in \mathbb{R}^{I_1 \times I_2 \times \cdots \times I_N}$ denotes the unknown tensor, $\mathcal{Y} \in \mathbb{R}^{I_1 \times I_2 \times \cdots \times I_N}$ represents the observed tensor, $\Omega$ is the index set of observed entries, and $\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}$ is the projection operator that retains entries within $\Omega$ while zeroing out others. The objective function $\|\mathcal{X}\|_{*}$ denotes the tensor nuclear norm, defined as the sum of the singular values of the mode-$n$ unfolding of $\mathcal{X}$ for each $n \in \{1, 2, \ldots, N\}$.

