\section{Introduction}

The rapid advancement of autonomous systems has sparked significant interest in developing a unified framework for modeling and analyzing their interactions. This paper proposes a new approach to modeling autonomous systems as a network of interacting agents, where each agent is defined by a set of variables and a corresponding set of rules. We demonstrate that this framework can be effectively applied to analyze the interactions between autonomous vehicles and their surrounding environment. Furthermore, we present a method for verifying the safety of these interactions, which is based on the concept of a "safe state." This state is characterized by the absence of collisions and the satisfaction of all safety constraints. Finally, we illustrate the application of this framework through a case study involving a single autonomous vehicle. The results indicate that the proposed framework is both effective and efficient for modeling and analyzing the interactions of autonomous systems. Additionally, the framework offers a promising avenue for future research into the safety verification of autonomous systems.

