Energy consumption forecasting is a critical component of energy management systems, enabling the optimization of energy usage and cost reduction. Accurate predictions allow for better scheduling of energy-intensive activities and the identification of opportunities for energy savings. This paper presents a hybrid model that combines deep learning and physics-based techniques to improve short-term energy consumption predictions. The deep learning aspect employs an LSTM network to model temporal patterns in energy data, while the physics-based aspect incorporates the correlation between energy use and weather conditions. The hybrid model is assessed using real data from a Dutch residential building. Findings show that the hybrid approach surpasses both the individual deep learning and physics-based models, delivering a 10% improvement in MAPE over the deep learning model. Additionally, the hybrid model excels in forecasting energy usage under extreme weather scenarios. The source code is available at https://github.com/ahmedmohamed1999/Hybrid-Model-for-Energy-Consumption-Prediction.

